1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,937
Welcome to Module One.

2
00:00:01,937 --> 00:00:04,375
You already know how evolution works.

3
00:00:04,375 --> 00:00:11,337
Variation, selection and inheritance, repeated until a population climbs a fitness landscape.

4
00:00:11,337 --> 00:00:20,475
Today you will see that training a large language model is the same kind of search, turned upside down, and you will also see exactly where that comparison breaks.

5
00:00:20,475 --> 00:00:22,237
Start with the landscape.

6
00:00:22,237 --> 00:00:26,037
In your field, height is fitness, and populations climb.

7
00:00:26,037 --> 00:00:28,375
In machine learning, height is loss.

8
00:00:28,375 --> 00:00:32,112
Loss is a single number that says how wrong the model is.

9
00:00:32,112 --> 00:00:35,312
So instead of climbing, the model descends.

10
00:00:35,312 --> 00:00:36,787
What is it wrong about?

11
00:00:36,787 --> 00:00:38,487
Something very simple.

12
00:00:38,487 --> 00:00:42,987
Take real text, hide the next word, and ask the model to guess it.

13
00:00:42,987 --> 00:00:45,500
If the guess is good, little changes.

14
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:52,150
If the guess is bad, that error becomes a signal, and the model is nudged so that next time it guesses better.

15
00:00:52,150 --> 00:00:56,737
Do that across a vast amount of text, and the guesses get remarkably good.

16
00:00:56,737 --> 00:00:59,175
The model's genotype is its weights.

17
00:00:59,175 --> 00:01:00,675
Billions of numbers.

18
00:01:00,675 --> 00:01:05,225
The landscape is the loss over every possible setting of those numbers.

19
00:01:05,225 --> 00:01:08,150
Here is the first big difference from evolution.

20
00:01:08,150 --> 00:01:12,512
A population discovers which way is uphill by trial and error.

21
00:01:12,512 --> 00:01:15,900
Mutants scatter, selection keeps the lucky ones.

22
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:18,925
Training does something no organism can do.

23
00:01:18,925 --> 00:01:21,125
It calculates the slope directly.

24
00:01:21,125 --> 00:01:26,262
For every single weight, it works out which small change would lower the loss.

25
00:01:26,262 --> 00:01:31,687
That set of slopes is called the gradient, and following it downhill is called gradient descent.

26
00:01:31,687 --> 00:01:36,050
In today's widget you will race two strategies across the same landscape.

27
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:37,750
One follows the slope.

28
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:44,887
The other is a simple evolutionary strategy that makes a handful of mutants each generation and keeps the best.

29
00:01:44,887 --> 00:01:47,875
Watch what happens when you make the landscape more rugged.

30
00:01:47,875 --> 00:01:53,162
Watch what happens when the step size is too big, or the mutations too small.

31
00:01:53,162 --> 00:01:57,175
You will find that each method fails in its own characteristic way.

32
00:01:57,175 --> 00:02:01,637
Then comes the reading, and one habit I want you to build from day one.

33
00:02:01,637 --> 00:02:06,400
Some of the sources in this course are excellent, and some are hype wrapped around a good idea.

34
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:15,250
One article today has a lovely explanation of optimization, and also some very precise numbers about a frontier model that nobody can trace.

35
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:16,525
Keep the idea.

36
00:02:16,525 --> 00:02:17,837
Drop the numbers.

37
00:02:17,837 --> 00:02:19,537
Finally, the lab.

38
00:02:19,537 --> 00:02:32,187
You will ask your coding agent, Claude Code or Codex, to write a small script that runs the race fifty times, from fifty random starting points, and reports how often each strategy finds the deepest valley.

39
00:02:32,187 --> 00:02:41,075
By the end of today you should be able to explain, in plain words, why training a model is optimisation, not guessing, and why gradients beat mutation at this scale.

40
00:02:41,075 --> 00:02:43,962
Open the widget, and let's start climbing down.

